## Notes for the reviewer

- **Where the golden digests and migration pins came from.** This agent has no shell. The two golden SHA-256 digests in test/event-hash.test.ts and the fourteen migration/snapshot digests in test/event-migration-additive.test.ts were produced by a read-only delegated sub-agent, not computed by hand: the golden digests with Node's `crypto.createHash("sha256")` over the plan's exact canonical bytes (`"v1\n" + JSON.stringify([...])`, timed case 85 UTF-8 bytes — matching the byte count derived by hand independently — and an all-day case of 71 bytes), without reading any production code; the migration digests from `git show main:<path>` and confirmed equal to the worktree files, with zero carriage-return bytes in every blob. None of this was executed by the test-writer itself, and no test in this suite was run. `test-reviewer`'s dynamic RED check is the first execution; if a pinned digest is wrong it will show up there as a failure that is not a missing-module failure.
- **Clean-up for the new tables, independent of the Implementer's `test/isolation.ts` change (plan Task 9).** test/event-schema.test.ts registers `beforeEach(resetEventTables, DRAIN_BUDGET_MS)` where `resetEventTables` first calls the existing `resetTaskTables()` (which drains every abandoned test body BEFORE wiping, the barrier docs/context/testing.md forbids hand-rolling) and then deletes `event_sync_links` then `events` through `env.DB`. Tests use `isolatedIt as it`, so their bodies are registered with the barrier. After Task 9 lands the two extra deletes are redundant and harmless. test/event-sync-links-fresh.test.ts deliberately has NO clean-up: per-file storage isolation plus `apply-migrations.ts` mean its first read sees exactly what the migration left, and any clean-up would make the "zero rows" assertion vacuous.
- **Why AC-6 and AC-7 use raw SQL, not Drizzle.** The PRD says "inserted directly, bypassing every route" and "rejected by SQL". `text(..., { enum })` is TypeScript-only, so a Drizzle insert could not even express `source = 'outlook'`. Raw SQL also keeps the suite from importing schema exports that do not exist yet, so the RED reason is "no such table", not a TypeScript error in the test. Each refused row is a known-good row (`TIMED` or `ALL_DAY`, inserted successfully by the "accepts" cases) with exactly one thing broken, so a refusal is attributable.
- **Plan-fixed names only.** The suite imports exactly the names the plan's Tasks 1-5 fix: `EVENT_TITLE_MAX`, `EVENT_DESCRIPTION_MAX`, `EVENT_LOCATION_MAX`, `EVENT_DEFAULT_DURATION_SECONDS`, `EVENT_DEFAULT_TIMEZONE`, `EVENT_SOURCES`, `EventSource`, `eventCapabilities`, `assertNeverEventSource`, `wallClockToInstant`, `instantToWallClock`, `isValidLocalDay`, `shiftLocalDay`, `displayEndDay`, `allDaySpanFromInclusive`, `isValidTimeZone`, `resolveEventTimes`, `validateEventInput`, `EventInput`, `ResolvedEvent` from `src/shared/events`; `computeEventContentHash` and `EventHashInput` from `src/shared/event-hash`. Table and column names in the SQL are the snake_case names Task 6 and the PRD Architecture Notes fix (`all_day`, `start_at`, `end_at`, `start_date`, `end_date`, `timezone`, `source`, `content_hash`, `event_id`, `calendar_id`, `external_event_id`, `etag`, `synced_content_hash`, `synced_at`). No constraint, index or function name that the PRD/plan do not fix is asserted. The `description`/`location` length boundaries are driven from the exported constants rather than literals, because the PRD's Open Questions call those numbers arbitrary; only the PRD-named numbers (title 200/201, default duration 3600) are pinned as literals.
- **AC-5 has a compile-time half that no runtime test can observe.** "Adding a value stops compiling" is covered from the test side by `expectTypeOf<EventSource>().toEqualTypeOf<"local" | "google">()` and `expectTypeOf(eventCapabilities).parameter(0).toEqualTypeOf<EventSource>()`, which `tsc -b` (`npm run check`) enforces — widening the union breaks this file's type check — plus the runtime fact that a source outside the union throws instead of returning `undefined`. The production-side `never` assertion in the switch default is the Implementer's own compile-time guarantee (plan Task 1, mirrored from `assertNeverDaySource`).
- **AC-33's frozen source files are deliberately not pinned by digest.** `src/shared/dates.ts`, `recurrence.ts` and `adherence.ts` are "unchanged for this unit", not frozen forever (unit 17 reuses `recurrence.ts`; later units may legitimately edit `dates.ts`), so a permanent digest test would break them for no reason. The plan proves the fact with `git diff --exit-code` against the fork point (Task 2, Task 10, Level 2). The one permanent behavioural guard added is in test/event-wall-clock.test.ts: the shipped `localPartsToInstant` still answers an ordinary time and still gives its own (different) gap answer, which is what Reminders depend on. For the same reason test/event-migration-additive.test.ts asserts exactly one `0007_*.sql` but not the absence of a `0008`.
- **Beyond-the-PRD-text cases, each justified by the plan.** (1) `resolveEventTimes` / `validateEventInput` error codes `required`, `not_allowed`, `invalid_date` and the fixed first-failure order are fixed by plan Task 4 and underlie phase 2's AC-10; (2) the shape-completeness refusals in AC-6 (timed row missing `start_at`/`end_at`, all-day row missing a date, `all_day` outside 0/1) follow from the `events_shape_chk` the plan specifies and close the "CHECK passes on NULL" hole the plan's Risks name; (3) tab/newline-only titles are refused by SQL because plan Task 6 specifies `trim(title, char(32, 9, 10, 13))` so the database agrees with the JavaScript validator's `trim()`; (4) the same external id under a different calendar is accepted, because the unique index is on the PAIR (plan Task 6, PRD D-H); (5) Southern-Hemisphere DST vectors (Australia/Sydney) and the times just inside/outside the New York gap and overlap in AC-1.
- **Foreign-key enforcement in the test D1.** The `ON DELETE SET NULL` assertions in AC-7 assume D1 enforces foreign keys (it does by default). If the link row keeps pointing at a deleted event when this runs, that is a finding about the test database (the plan's Risks table says to raise it), not a reason to loosen the test.
